1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,138
In the 1960s,

2
00:00:01,138 --> 00:00:02,357
the sugar industry quietly

3
00:00:02,357 --> 00:00:04,061
hired Harvard scientists.

4
00:00:04,061 --> 00:00:05,350
Their job?

5
00:00:05,350 --> 00:00:07,657
Review what really causes heart disease.

6
00:00:07,657 --> 00:00:10,409
Early studies were starting to point at sugar.

7
00:00:10,409 --> 00:00:11,857
The industry was worried.

8
00:00:11,857 --> 00:00:13,018
So it picked the question,

9
00:00:13,018 --> 00:00:15,540
and mailed over the studies it wanted challenged.

10
00:00:15,540 --> 00:00:17,328
One scientist wrote back:

11
00:00:17,328 --> 00:00:20,025
"We are well aware of your particular interest."

12
00:00:20,025 --> 00:00:22,788
The review ran in a top medical journal.

13
00:00:22,788 --> 00:00:24,309
Sugar? Played down.

14
00:00:24,309 --> 00:00:26,250
Fat? The main target.

15
00:00:26,250 --> 00:00:28,105
Nobody told readers who paid for it.

16
00:00:28,105 --> 00:00:30,241
Fifty years later, in 2016,

17
00:00:30,241 --> 00:00:33,211
researchers found the letters in old archives.

18
00:00:33,211 --> 00:00:35,011
And the price of that review?

19
00:00:35,011 --> 00:00:37,373
Just $6,500.

20
00:00:37,373 --> 00:00:39,788
No one proved the money changed a word.

21
00:00:39,788 --> 00:00:41,413
But the science had a sponsor,

22
00:00:41,413 --> 00:00:42,750
and nobody was told.
